Now I understand the P2B design: `scheduler()` only modifies PCB state and current_running (no context switch — that's done by do_scheduler entry.S wrapper and ret_from_exception).

Classic P2B scheduler implementation:

```c
void scheduler(void)
{
    pcb_t *prev_running = current_running;
    pcb_t *next_running;

    if (prev_running->status == TASK_RUNNING) {
        prev_running->status = TASK_READY;
        list_add_tail(&prev_running->list, &ready_queue);
        prev_running->ready_tick = get_ticks();
    }

    list_node_t *next_node = max_priority_node();
    next_running = list_entry(next_node, pcb_t, list);
    list_del(next_node);
    next_running->status = TASK_RUNNING;
    current_running = next_running;
    process_id = next_running->pid; // maybe not

    screen_move_cursor(...); // restore cursor
}
```

max_priority_node: iterate ready_queue, find node with highest priority; tie-break by earliest ready_tick (FCFS among equal priority).

```c
list_node_t* max_priority_node(void)
{
    list_node_t *p, *max_node = ready_queue.next;
    uint8_t max_priority = list_entry(max_node, pcb_t, list)->priority;
    for (p = ready_queue.next; p != &ready_queue; p = p->next) {
        pcb_t *pcb = list_entry(p, pcb_t, list);
        if (pcb->priority > max_priority)
            ...
        else if (pcb->priority == max_priority && pcb->ready_tick < ...->ready_tick)
    }
    return max_node;
}
```

Note ready_queue might be empty? pid0 is always in the queue effectively... In scheduler, if ready queue empty and prev blocked... For safety, if list_empty, keep current_running? Classic implementation assumes non-empty. But if all tasks sleep, pid0 loops in main calling do_scheduler. Hmm, pid0's status when re-added: TASK_RUNNING → TASK_READY → requeued. So queue non-empty whenever scheduler called from do_scheduler path with running task. When called from do_sleep: current was blocked (not requeued) — ready queue could theoretically be empty except pid0 might be blocked too? pid0 never blocks. OK.

Edge: timer expired during scheduler? timer_check is called in irq.c (timer interrupt handler). Fine.

Note in P2B, is `do_scheduler` (C version) needed? entry.S defines do_scheduler as asm symbol. The header declares it; no C definition needed. Good — the acceptance signal `do_scheduler` is satisfied by entry.S.

timer_check: iterate timer_queue; for each timer with timeout_tick <= get_ticks(), remove from queue and call callback(parameter).

```c
void timer_check()
{
    uint64_t current_tick = get_ticks();
    list_node_t *node = timer_queue.next;
    while (node != &timer_queue) {
        timer_t *timer = list_entry(node, timer_t, list);
        list_node_t *next = node->next;
        if (timer->timeout_tick <= current_tick) {
            list_del(node);
            timer->callback_func(timer->parameter);
        }
        node = next;
    }
}
